Week 3 — Application Selection for Performance Testing
← Week 2 | Week 3 | Week 4 →
Overview
Week 3 focuses on selecting applications that represent different workload types for performance evaluation. The aim is to test how the Linux server behaves under varied CPU, memory, disk, and network loads. Application selection ensures a representative evaluation of system performance and supports optimisation in later weeks.
All installations and configurations are carried out remotely via SSH from the workstation to the headless server.
Objectives
Identify applications for testing different workload types
Classify applications by resource intensity (CPU, memory, I/O, network)
Document installation procedures and commands
Predict expected resource usage for each application
Define monitoring strategies for each workload
Application Selection Matrix
Application	Workload Type	Justification for Selection
stress-ng	CPU-intensive	Simulates high CPU load, configurable tests for benchmarking
memtester	Memory-intensive	Tests RAM allocation and limits under load
fio	Disk I/O-intensive	Measures read/write performance for storage analysis
iperf3	Network-intensive	Evaluates network throughput and latency
nginx	Server workload	Representative server application for response times and concurrency testing
Installation Documentation
All installations were executed remotely via SSH on the server. Example commands:
# Update package list
sudo apt update

# CPU stress tool
sudo apt install -y stress-ng

# Memory testing tool
sudo apt install -y memtester

# Disk I/O benchmark tool
sudo apt install -y fio

# Network testing tool
sudo apt install -y iperf3

# Web server
sudo apt install -y nginx
Notes:
All commands were executed on 192.168.56.103 from the workstation using SSH.
sudo is used to execute administrative installations safely.
Packages installed are LTS-supported versions from Ubuntu repositories.
